WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.959 --> 00:00:06.750
If there’s one thing I appreciate when playing
video games, it’s great mechanics.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:12.391
The web swinging in Spiderman: Miles Morales
feels really natural, and in Fortnite, 

00:00:12.391 --> 00:00:17.730
you can destroy essentially everything, and then
turn it into a wall, with a few intuitive gestures.

00:00:18.026 --> 00:00:22.062
But The Last of Us Part 2 impressed me for
something much more subtle 

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:25.751
The doors.

00:00:25.914 --> 00:00:31.302
This mundane, everyday task is what took developers
the longest to get right,

00:00:31.697 --> 00:00:36.829
As the games’ co-director put it on Twitter:
“f--k doors”

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:42.000
The Last of Us Part II is a super realistic
game, and the team knew that “If a player

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.329
is going to open a door, it can’t just magically
fly open.”

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:53.769
But even for less realistic games like Fortnite,
doors are still painstakingly hard to make.

00:00:53.769 --> 00:00:58.552
So, what makes video game doors so difficult?

00:01:01.584 --> 00:01:05.780
Every time I'd walk through a doorway, I just
keep, like, bonking myself into the corners

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.580
and into the walls next to the doors.

00:01:07.893 --> 00:01:10.704
It's like, all right, well, this sucks.

00:01:10.704 --> 00:01:14.836
I'm Brian Singh, I’m a game developer and
I guess I really like doors.

00:01:14.836 --> 00:01:20.180
Bryan’s worked on games, like Journey, Uncharted
4, and The Last Of Us Parts 1 &amp; 2.

00:01:20.509 --> 00:01:24.340
On Part 2, it was his job to get doors working,
from a technical perspective.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:31.270
I think the complexity of all different door
states and ways of interacting with doors,

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:35.560
it seems simple in the real world 
because you're used to it.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.869
But in a video game, that’s not the case:

00:01:37.869 --> 00:01:43.039
You have to consider every other situation,
every other system, every other object and

00:01:43.039 --> 00:01:47.969
character that can interact with doors or
be around doors when they're interacting

00:01:47.969 --> 00:01:50.290
Start counting the number of doors 
you actually see in games.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.878
You'll see a lot of games just don't put doors
in the doorways.

00:01:53.536 --> 00:01:58.132
This ship in World of Warcraft has just an
open doorway.

00:01:58.132 --> 00:02:00.363
So does this room in Assassin's Creed.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:07.585
For games that do have doors, they fall into
a few unofficial difficulty categories.

00:02:09.707 --> 00:02:17.140
Games like Halo use tier one doors: they open
or close based on your proximity to them.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.634
These doors are the least finicky to implement.

00:02:21.751 --> 00:02:25.566
They don’t interact with anything, because they slide in on themselves,

00:02:25.566 --> 00:02:30.197
and they have no in-between state - 
they’re only open or closed.

00:02:31.447 --> 00:02:34.848
Tier 2 doors have to look like real-world

00:02:34.848 --> 00:02:38.420
doors...even if they don’t operate in exactly
the same way.

00:02:38.848 --> 00:02:44.066
SINGH: It's very uncommon in the real world to have
a door that opens in both directions.

00:02:44.066 --> 00:02:47.085
In video games, it's extremely common.

00:02:47.397 --> 00:02:49.844
The character in the game usually doesn’t 

00:02:49.844 --> 00:02:55.945
physically touch the door in this tier, but
sometimes, like in Fortnite, developers might

00:02:55.945 --> 00:02:59.413
throw in an arm gesture to suggest 
character interaction.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.730
SINGH: One of the things that comes up a lot in games
is, it's kind of assumed that the size of

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:08.940
doors are going to be exaggerated because
you have this problem: What your character

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:13.465
is doing is not at all what the actual human
player is doing behind the controller.

00:03:13.704 --> 00:03:20.000
Precision movements are challenging, which
would make navigating through a small door frustrating.

00:03:20.472 --> 00:03:25.430
SINGH: Usually the default solution is: just make
the doors really big, and that way it’s

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:28.020
way easier to go through the doors, you don’t
have to think about it.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:33.590
But that’s not the only reason doors are
huge - they also allow for cleaner visuals

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:36.490
SINGH: Once your player goes through the door, like,
what is the camera doing?

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:39.140
Normally the camera is like, slightly above
door height.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:42.720
SINGH: So you have to, like, duck it down to make
sure the cameraman also squeezes through the

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.932
door and that doesn't look like crazy.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.759
 Doors also have to look like they’re physically 

00:03:47.759 --> 00:03:50.280
interacting with everything else in the scene.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.780
Animation and doors already f--ing complicated problem.

00:03:53.944 --> 00:04:00.084
In real life, everything is solid - but in the world of 3D modeling, nothing is solid.

00:04:00.084 --> 00:04:03.750
Objects can pass right through each other. 

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:08.490
Making sure that doesn’t happen in every
possible scenario, with any possible object

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.586
is a tedious task.

00:04:11.586 --> 00:04:17.169
Tier 3 doors are maximum effort: The character
has to actually physically interact

00:04:17.169 --> 00:04:19.555
with the door for it to open.

00:04:19.637 --> 00:04:24.090
Having a character actually connect with the
handle of the door, without breaking player

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:29.629
immersion, is a complicated dance between
player control and pre-set animations

00:04:29.892 --> 00:04:32.280
It's pretty much a golden rule.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.092
Any time you take control away from the player,
it feels weird, it feels bad.

00:04:36.092 --> 00:04:37.532
So with doors...

00:04:37.532 --> 00:04:41.480
Worst case scenario, you hit the button and
you're effectively playing a cut scene or something.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.449
Giving players enough control to break away

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:47.680
from the door in the middle of opening it
means that character animation must be chopped

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:50.000
up into tiny pieces.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.188
But even if you get all that right, the work
doesn’t end there.

00:04:54.188 --> 00:05:00.360
Any time you add any one thing to the gameplay,
you have to make sure it works with every

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:04.480
other aspect of the game.
For instance, characters can have different

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.840
emotional states that require unique animations.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:13.231
If you’re calmly investigating a room for
example, you’ll open the door more slowly.

00:05:13.231 --> 00:05:19.462
If they're in a like, tense demeanor, like,
oh, crap, there's enemies here, I'm going to go,

00:05:19.462 --> 00:05:22.916
they can run through the door with
great haste.

00:05:22.916 --> 00:05:28.423
Throw in a few NPCs, and the meticulous work
just gets even more complicated.

00:05:28.423 --> 00:05:31.569
SINGH: A.I. is a whole other complexity where.

00:05:31.569 --> 00:05:36.750
OK, now the companion characters, as well
as the enemy characters, also have to understand

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:42.550
how to strategize around that, like, OK, do
they test the door to learn that it's locked?

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:46.949
Do they know that it's locked already and
know how to, like, try to find a way around

00:05:46.949 --> 00:05:48.159
the door?

00:05:48.159 --> 00:05:54.680
And this problem isn’t just localized to
doors, other objects like ladders are also

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.584
challenging to make, for similar reasons.

00:05:57.584 --> 00:06:02.369
Doors are hard because all of game development
is hard.

00:06:02.369 --> 00:06:07.189
But when the goal of a game is realism and
the fidelity that you can achieve with new

00:06:07.189 --> 00:06:13.370
consoles is incredible...that challenge becomes
something of a necessity. 

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:19.590
SINGH:  Especially on the last of us, the philosophy
was: avoid as many disconnects from reality

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:20.590
as possible.

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:25.030
So you can just be more immersed, like everytime
you experience a disconnect, that's just like

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:29.069
one more reminder that, oh, yeah, I'm playing
a video game instead of being invested into

00:06:29.069 --> 00:06:30.756
what's going on. 

00:06:31.965 --> 00:06:35.779
In researching this story, I came across this 2014

00:06:35.779 --> 00:06:40.340
post by video game designer Liz England, called
The Door Problem.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:46.020
In it, she breaks down just how much of a
pain doors are for designers, with a series

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:51.030
of questions that need to be answered before
work even begins on a game:

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:54.719
It starts off fairly simple: Are there doors
in your game?

00:06:54.719 --> 00:06:55.879
Can the player open them?

00:06:55.879 --> 00:07:01.439
But pretty quickly it gets a lot more complicated:
Do doors lock behind the player?

00:07:01.439 --> 00:07:02.680
Where do enemies come from?

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:04.710
Do they run in from doors?

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:07.210
What happens if there are two players?

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:12.309
Once you get all those questions answered,
the door then actually has to get built — and

00:07:12.309 --> 00:07:16.286
every part of the company gets involved in
a different way:

00:07:16.286 --> 00:07:18.319
Concept artists design and create,

00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:19.979
composers write themes,

00:07:19.979 --> 00:07:22.169
combat designers think about enemies,

00:07:22.169 --> 00:07:23.520
writers write voice over,

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.659
sound designers make it sound like the door
is actually opening and closing, and then

00:07:27.659 --> 00:07:32.419
an audio engineer has to come in and make
it sound like it's coming from the direction

00:07:32.419 --> 00:07:36.909
that the player is facing, and the list goes
on.

00:07:36.909 --> 00:07:41.372
For anyone who's interestedI’ll put the link to her post in the description below.

00:07:41.372 --> 00:07:46.876
But, the expert that I interviewed for this piece, Bryan Singh,
had very similar things to say:

00:07:46.876 --> 00:07:49.669
Doors are like, yeah, you open the door, you're
done.

00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:55.690
It's like, yes, but you have to consider every
other situation, every other system, every

00:07:55.690 --> 00:08:00.759
other object and character that can interact
with doors or be around doors when they're

00:08:00.759 --> 00:08:01.759
interacting.

00:08:01.759 --> 00:08:05.210
Like, what if you open the door and there's
an enemy right behind it?

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:07.370
Do you have an animation for that?

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:12.300
Does the enemy get bonked and knocked back,
or do they like stop it?

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:14.499
Those are questions that you have to ask yourself.

00:08:14.499 --> 00:08:21.759
Like, how does every situation or every overlapping
system react to push button, door opens.

